What is Disaster Recovery (DR)?
      Disaster Recovery (DR) means bringing your application back after something goes wrong.

RTO = How long your application can be down
        ex-Example:

                    Your website stops working at 2:00 PM.
                    
                    You want it back by 2:15 PM.
                    
                    So,
                    
                    RTO = 15 minutes
                    
                    Smaller RTO means:
                    
                    Faster recovery
                    More cost
RPO = How much data you can afford to lose.
      Example:

                    Your database saves a copy every 10 minutes.
                    
                    The last backup was at 1:50 PM.
                    
                    The server crashes at 2:00 PM.
                    
                    You lose 10 minutes of data.
                    
                    So,
                    
                    RPO = 10 minutes
                    
                    Smaller RPO means:
                    
                    Less data loss
                    More frequent backups
                    Higher cost


========================

Four Disaster Recovery Strategies

                    1. Backup and Restore (Cheapest)
                            Pros
                            Lowest cost
                            Easy to set up
                            Cons
                            Takes a long time to recover
                            Users wait longer
                            
                            Best for:
                            
                            Small websites
                            Internal tools
                            Applications that can be down for a few hours


                     2. Pilot Light

                              Think of a gas stove.
                              
                              The small flame (pilot light) is always on.
                              
                              In AWS, only the most important parts stay running.
                              
                              For example:
                              
                              Running:
                              
                              Database
                              Storage
                              Network
                              
                              Not running:
                              
                              Application servers
                              Extra EC2 instances
                              
                              If disaster happens:
                           ==============   
                              Start EC2 servers
                              
                              ↓
                              
                              Deploy application
                              
                              ↓
                              
                              Connect to database
                              
                              ↓
                              
                              Application is ready
                            ==================
                              Pros
                              Faster than Backup & Restore
                              Lower cost than Warm Standby
                              Cons
                              Still takes a few minutes to start everything

                            3. Warm Standby

                              A small version of your application is always running.
                              
                              Example:
                              
                              Production:
                              
                              10 EC2 servers
                              
                              Backup:
                              ==================
                              1 EC2 server
                              
                              If disaster happens:
                              
                              1 EC2
                              
                              ↓
                              
                              Auto Scaling
                              
                              ↓
                              
                              10 EC2
                              
                              Because the application is already running, AWS only needs to add more servers.
                              ===================
                              Pros
                              Fast recovery
                              Easy to test
                              Cons
                              Costs more because backup servers are always running

                            4. Multi-site Active/Active

                              This is the best but most expensive option.
                              
                              Two AWS Regions run the application at the same time.
                              
                              Example:
                              if one refion gets disaster still another serves- banks, e-commerce, healthcare , important bussiness applications
